Heh.  I understand that you have made this decision for game
balance.  Nail it down by making sure your descriptions of safes
are clear about that point though, or people will assume that
the use of safes (a 19th century technology ANYWAY) refer to
early 19th-century type safes, which did in fact use keys.

Burglar-resistent safes: First produced by the Chubb company
in 1818, and patented in 1835 by Charles and Jeremiah Chubb
(Prior to that you had nonportable vaults and strongrooms).
Single-dial combination lock: invented and patented 1862 by
Linus Yale, Jr.

The earliest safes (and a lot of later ones, including most
made today for non-bank applications, like the iron box under
the counter of your local taco bell and the document safe in
the office of your local bar, etc) in fact have key locks.

Of course, this brief search reveals that both safes and that
type of combination lock come far later than any point at which
people were wearing plate armor and using swords.

Multiple-dial combination locks (you see them today on bike locks,
etc) on the other hand were described in 1206 by Al-Jazari in a
book whose title translates into English as "The Book of Knowledge
of Ingenious Mechanical Devices."  So, hey, your safe could have
those.

